-- Passage rg7-main-idea-summary-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('3d121767-9c86-5478-99a1-76c759eb12e4', 'rg7-main-idea-summary-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Hidden Workers in a Compost Pile$q$, $q$A backyard compost pile can look like a heap of trash, but inside it a busy community of living things is hard at work. Composting is the process of breaking down food scraps, leaves and other once-living material into a dark, crumbly substance that helps plants grow. Understanding how it works can help anyone turn waste into something useful.

The real workers in a compost pile are tiny. Bacteria and fungi feed on the scraps and break them into simpler materials. Larger creatures such as earthworms, beetles and pill bugs tear the material into smaller pieces, which gives the microbes more surface to work on.

These organisms need the right conditions to do their job. They need a mix of "greens," such as fruit peels and grass clippings, and "browns," such as dry leaves and shredded paper. Greens supply nitrogen, while browns supply carbon. The pile also needs air and moisture. A gardener who turns the pile with a shovel every week or so lets in fresh oxygen. A pile that feels like a damp sponge has about the right amount of water.

When conditions are good, the center of the pile heats up. The microbes release heat as they work, and a healthy pile can become warm enough to steam on a cool morning. This heat speeds up the process and kills many weed seeds.

After a few months, the scraps are gone and the finished compost smells like soil. Gardeners mix it into flower beds and vegetable gardens to add nutrients and help the ground hold water. In this way, composting keeps material out of landfills and returns it to the earth.$q$, 'draft')
