-- Passage rg7-main-idea-summary-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('053f4100-8760-5997-b40c-6f3621becd51', 'rg7-main-idea-summary-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Borrowing a Drill Like a Book$q$, $q$When Rosa Delgado needed a ladder to clean her gutters, she did not buy one. She borrowed it from the Millbrook Tool Library, a small shop on Elm Street where residents check out tools the way they check out books.

The tool library opened four years ago in an empty storefront. A group of neighbors noticed that many families owned expensive tools they used only once or twice a year, while other families could not afford them at all. They asked people to donate tools they rarely used. Within a few months, the shelves held drills, rakes, saws, pressure washers and even a cider press.

Borrowing works much like a regular library. Members sign up with a library card, pay a small yearly fee and can take home up to three tools for one week. Volunteers check each tool when it comes back, sharpen blades and replace worn parts.

The program also teaches skills. On Saturday mornings, volunteers such as Kwame Asante, a retired carpenter, lead free workshops on fixing a leaky faucet or building a simple bookshelf. Many members say these classes gave them the confidence to tackle repairs they once paid others to do.

The tool library has helped Millbrook in other ways, too. Sharing tools means fewer items are bought, stored and eventually thrown away. Neighbors who meet at workshops often end up helping one another with projects. Last spring, a group of members used borrowed tools to rebuild the benches at Riverside Park.

Other towns have started to notice. Organizers from two nearby communities visited this fall to learn how Millbrook set up its program. For a town that started with a few donated rakes, the tool library has become a model of how sharing can save money and bring people together.$q$, 'draft')
