-- Passage rg7-main-idea-summary-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('57630e0b-aecf-53bc-a0c0-e360656fc581', 'rg7-main-idea-summary-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Fighting Sound with Sound$q$, $q$On a noisy bus or airplane, some headphones can make the world seem almost silent. These noise-canceling headphones do not simply block sound. Instead, they use tiny microphones and quick calculations to fight sound with sound.

To understand how this works, it helps to know what sound is. Sound travels through the air as waves of pressure. Each wave has high points, where the air is squeezed together, and low points, where the air is spread apart. Our ears detect these changes in pressure and send signals to the brain.

Noise-canceling headphones contain small microphones on the outside of each ear cup. These microphones pick up the steady sounds around the listener, such as the hum of an engine. A tiny computer chip inside the headphones then creates a new sound wave that is the exact opposite of the noise. Where the noise wave has a high point, the new wave has a low point.

When the two waves meet, they cancel each other out. Scientists call this effect destructive interference. The listener hears much less of the engine, while music or a voice from the headphones comes through clearly.

The technology has limits. It works best on low, steady sounds like engines and air conditioners. Sudden or changing sounds, such as a dog barking or people talking, are harder to predict, so some of that noise still gets through. For this reason, most of these headphones also use thick, padded ear cups that physically block higher sounds. By combining padding with smart electronics, the headphones give listeners a quieter place to work, rest or enjoy music.$q$, 'draft')
